The period from 1538 to 1606 was one of the most tumultuous in the history of the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. The kingdom's colonial empire expanded across the [[New World]], generating unprecedented wealth but also destabilising the domestic economy. The flood of colonial goods into the Zong market produced a surplus crisis that devastated domestic producers, triggered a peasant revolution, and created the conditions for a [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] usurper — [[Foyuan of Zong|Emperor Foyuan]] of the [[House of Canglan]] — to seize the throne. The usurper's brief four-year reign, during which he scrapped the Tianist religious order, provoked immediate elite resistance, and led to the Yuanlong restoration that overthrew and executed him. The restored emperor, [[Tianren of Zong|Tianren]], pursued a policy of Baiduist integration that ended with his assassination in the [[Canglan Rebellion]]. His brother [[Tianxiao of Zong|Tianxiao]] inherited the throne and, convinced that coexistence was impossible, issued the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong]] of 1606.

The period unfolded against a backdrop of global upheaval: the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564, when [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] explorers discovered the Fosian colonies; the [[Sengoku Period|Sengoku Export]] of Sanese mercenaries; the [[Secret Trade]] in enslaved peoples; the [[War of the Mesogean]] between the Illypnian colonial powers; and the rise of the [[Sultanate of Maros]] as a Fosian colonial competitor.

== The First Lurandian Native Wars (1538–1560s) ==

{{Main|First Lurandian Native Wars}}

The colonial split of 1543, which divided the Zong colonial territory into the [[Sinchew]] and [[Louyang]] colonies along the [[Qingjiang]] river, did not end the conflicts that had produced it. Both colonies faced sustained indigenous resistance throughout the mid-16th century, as the expansion of Zong settlements, plantations, and trading networks encroached on the territories of indigenous peoples.

The indigenous wars were not a single conflict but a series of overlapping campaigns, uprisings, and retaliatory actions that varied by region. In [[Louyang]], the older and more established colony, the indigenous resistance was largely attritional — raids on outlying plantations, ambushes of supply convoys, and periodic assaults on isolated trading posts. The Louyang colonial administration, with its larger garrison and its closer relationship with the metropolitan court, was generally able to contain these threats without major escalation.

In [[Sinchew]], the situation was more severe. The northwestern colony was a frontier society — harsher, more militarised, and more remote from metropolitan support. The indigenous peoples of the region were more organised and more hostile to the Zong presence, and the Sinchew colonial administration was forced to conduct sustained military campaigns to secure its territory. The Sinchew garrison was supplemented by Sanese mercenaries — the [[Sea Wolves]], drawn from the dispossessed samurai of the [[Sengoku Period|Sengoku-era]] Sanese archipelago — who proved effective in the colonial warfare but also introduced a volatile element into the colonial society.

The wars were complicated by the involvement of the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] colonial empire. After the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564, the Volisanian colonial authorities in the eastern Lurandian colony of [[Balboa]] began smuggling firearms to indigenous Lurandian resistance groups through trans-isthmus trade networks. The Volisanian authorities, alarmed by the discovery of a well-organised Fosian colonial presence on the western coast, calculated that an armed indigenous resistance would weaken the Zong colonies and reduce the Fosian threat to Volisanian interests in the eastern hemisphere. The influx of Volisanian muskets into the western Lurandian interior intensified the indigenous wars, enabling indigenous forces to challenge the Zong garrisons in engagements that had previously been decided by the Zong military's superiority in discipline and weaponry.

The indigenous wars were costly, both in lives and in resources, and they placed a sustained burden on the metropolitan court. The Zong military was forced to maintain a permanent garrison presence in both colonies, and the cost of supplying these garrisons across the [[Shendan Ocean]] consumed a growing share of the court's revenue. The wars also produced a steady flow of casualties and displaced settlers returning to the Zong mainland, where their accounts of the colonial violence contributed to a growing public anxiety about the colonial enterprise.

== The Great Disappointment (1564) ==

{{Main|Great Disappointment}}

In 1564, the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] expedition of [[Berenguer de Montcada]] crossed the isthmus connecting [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]] and encountered the Zong colonial presence on the western coast of the New World. The expedition discovered Zong trading outposts and the major colony of [[Tamsui]], garrisoned by Sanese mercenaries whom the Volisanians called the "Shendan Demons."

The discovery produced a strategic and psychological crisis among the Illypnian colonial powers. The revelation that the New World was already being colonised by Fosian powers of comparable organisational capacity shattered the assumption that the western hemisphere was an uncontested frontier. For Zong, the consequences were equally significant: the colonial presence was now known to Western powers, and the strategic calculus of the Shendan enterprise had changed fundamentally.

The Great Disappointment accelerated Illypnian colonisation of Abyala, intensified colonial wars, and opened new trans-isthmus trade routes between the Illypnian and Fosian colonial spheres. It also introduced a lasting suspicion between the two civilisational spheres that would shape colonial relations for decades.

== The surplus crisis (1560s–1580s) ==

{{Main|Surplus crisis (Zong)}}

The colonial trade that had restored Zong's prosperity in the early 16th century became, by mid-century, the source of a new economic crisis. The expansion of the plantation economies in [[Sinchew]] and [[Louyang]] — and the growth of the trading networks that connected the colonies to the Zong mainland — produced a flood of colonial goods into the Zong market. Spices, sugar, tobacco, cotton, tropical hardwoods, and dyes arrived in quantities that exceeded the absorptive capacity of the domestic economy.

The surplus drove down prices. Colonial spices — pepper, cinnamon, nutmeg — which had been scarce and expensive during the blockade years, became cheap and abundant. For consumers, this was a benefit; for domestic producers, it was a catastrophe. Farmers in the northern provinces who had grown spices and cotton for the domestic market found themselves undercut by cheaper colonial imports. Artisans who had processed these raw materials — weavers, dyers, spice merchants — faced contracting demand as the colonial trade captured their markets.

The crisis deepened over two decades. As domestic producers were ruined, the agricultural sector contracted. Farmers abandoned their fields or switched to subsistence crops, reducing the kingdom's food self-sufficiency. The artisanal sector, which had been a major source of urban employment in the eastern port cities, entered a prolonged depression. Unemployment rose, tax revenue from domestic production fell, and the court's fiscal position — which had been rebuilt on the foundation of the Shendan trade — became increasingly dependent on colonial revenue at the expense of the domestic economy.

The surplus crisis also had a monetary dimension. The colonial trade generated large inflows of precious metals — gold and silver from the New World — that inflated the money supply while the domestic productive base contracted. The result was a combination of price deflation in agricultural and artisanal goods and inflation in land and luxury assets, a pattern that enriched the colonial merchant elite while impoverishing the domestic producing classes.

The [[Societal Assembly]] was divided over the response. The Maritime Faction, now dominated by the colonial merchant houses, argued that the surplus was a temporary adjustment and that the long-term benefits of the colonial trade outweighed the short-term costs. The Continental Faction, weakened but not eliminated, argued that the crisis demonstrated the danger of over-reliance on colonial commerce and that the kingdom needed to protect its domestic producers. The court, under [[Tianhao of Zong|Emperor Tianhao]], was unable to forge a consensus, and the crisis festered.

== The Great Peasant Revolt (1572–1585) ==

{{Main|Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)}}

The surplus crisis produced the conditions for a major social upheaval. By the early 1570s, the combination of agricultural contraction, artisanal unemployment, and fiscal strain had created a large population of dispossessed and impoverished people in the agrarian interior and the eastern port cities. The revolt that erupted in 1572 was not a single event but a wave of uprisings that spread across the kingdom over thirteen years, ending only with the Baiduist usurpation of 1585.

=== Baiduist oppression ===

The religious dimension of the revolt was rooted in the systematic oppression of Baiduism by the Tianist establishment. Since the mid-16th century, Tianist officials in the western provinces had imposed increasingly harsh restrictions on Baiduist practice. Baiduist temples were subject to arbitrary closure, monastic ordination was restricted, and Baiduist communities were subjected to special taxation and surveillance. In some provinces, local officials banned Baiduist worship outright, forcing practitioners underground. The foreign Baiduist communities — Darasian monks, Shansian merchants, and their families — were subjected to periodic expulsion orders, confiscation of property, and violence from Tianist militias.

The oppression was not merely administrative. Tianist hardliners in the [[Societal Assembly]] argued that Baiduism was an Darasian import, a continental fifth column that threatened the imperial order. The rhetoric of the Tianist establishment increasingly framed Baiduism not as a legitimate religious alternative but as a subversive foreign ideology. Baiduist peasants in the western interior, who had been drawn to the religion through the missionary networks of the eastern monasteries, faced discrimination in the courts, in taxation, and in access to land. The combination of economic hardship from the surplus crisis and religious persecution created a volatile mix.

=== Baiduism as liberation ===

For the dispossessed peasantry, Baiduism increasingly came to represent freedom from oppression. The Baiduist teaching of the Ocean Crossing (海渡) — the idea that the hundred crossings were like a voyage across the sea, requiring both individual effort and the grace of favorable winds — resonated with a population that saw itself as trapped between an exploitative economic order and a persecutory religious establishment. The Baiduist monasteries of the eastern coast, which had grown wealthy through the Shendan trade, provided charity, education, and community organisation that the Tianist state had ceased to offer. Baiduist monks who travelled into the western provinces preached a message of spiritual equality and material relief that found a ready audience among the rural poor.

The Baiduist communities positioned themselves as defenders of the people against a failed Tianist order. The eastern monasteries offered sanctuary to refugees from the surplus crisis, distributed food and medicine during the famine years, and organised mutual aid networks that the imperial administration could not or would not provide. For many peasants, the choice between Tianism — the religion of the court that had allowed the surplus crisis and the religious oppression — and Baiduism — the religion of the communities that offered material relief and spiritual dignity — was not a theological choice but a practical one.

=== Course of the revolt ===

The revolt began in 1572 in the western provinces, where the surplus crisis had hit the agrarian economy hardest. Dispossessed farmers, accompanied by Baiduist peasants and supported by local Baiduist monasteries, attacked tax offices, granaries, and the estates of Tianist aristocrats. The uprisings spread to the eastern port cities, where unemployed artisans and dockworkers joined the revolt, attacking the warehouses and mansions of the colonial merchant houses.

=== The introduction of firearms ===

A pivotal factor in the revolt's success was the introduction of firearms into Zong's domestic warfare. Prior to the Great Peasant Revolt, the Zong military's use of gunpowder was limited to siege weapons and hand cannons — technologies adopted from Darasian naval vessels and primarily deployed in naval operations and colonial garrison defence. The Zong army, oriented toward the continental frontier and the colonial expeditions, had not extensively adopted handheld firearms for infantry combat.

The Baiduist militias, however, acquired matchlock muskets through smuggling networks that ran through [[Daras]] and the continental trade routes. The Baiduist monasteries of the eastern coast, with their extensive commercial connections to Darasian Baiduist institutions, were able to channel firearms into the hands of the rebel militias. The Darasian arms were superior in reliability and rate of fire to the hand cannons used by the imperial garrisons, and the Baiduist militias quickly developed tactics that exploited the mobility and firepower of the matchlock musket in the field.

The introduction of firearms transformed the military balance. The imperial army, which had expected to suppress the revolt with the same disciplinary and cavalry tactics that had served it in the colonial wars, was confronted with Baiduist militias that could match or exceed its firepower. The Zong military's hand cannons, designed for siege and naval use, were slow to reload and unwieldy in the field; the Darasian matchlocks, in the hands of the Baiduist militias, proved decisive in the engagements of the late 1570s and early 1580s. The court's inability to match the Baiduist firepower — a consequence of the military's orientation toward siege and naval gunpowder rather than infantry firearms — was a critical factor in the collapse of the imperial authority in the western provinces.

=== Collapse of imperial authority ===

The Zong military, stretched thin by the colonial garrisons in the New World and outgunned by the Baiduist militias, was unable to contain the revolt. [[Tianhao of Zong|Emperor Tianhao]] attempted to negotiate in 1575, offering tax reductions and debt relief, but the concessions were too little and too late. The revolt continued to escalate, and by the late 1570s the court's authority in the western provinces had effectively collapsed. Baiduist militias, organised and funded by the eastern monasteries and armed with Darasian firearms, became the dominant military force in several provinces, displacing the imperial garrisons and establishing a parallel administration that collected taxes, adjudicated disputes, and maintained order according to Baiduist principles.

The revolt ended not with a negotiated settlement but with the Baiduist usurpation. The Tianist establishment, which had been unable to prevent the surplus crisis or to contain the revolt, was discredited. The Baiduist communities, which had both the economic resources and the organisational networks to present an alternative, positioned themselves as the defenders of the people against a failed Tianist order. The [[House of Canglan]], the wealthiest and most influential Baiduist family in the kingdom, emerged as the political vehicle for this transformation.

== The Baiduist usurpation (c. 1585) ==

=== The House of Canglan ===

The [[House of Canglan]] (蒼蘭, "Azure Orchid") was a noble family of the eastern coast that had grown wealthy through the Shendan trade and had adopted [[Baiduism]] in the early 16th century. The family's commercial networks extended across the Shendan Ocean to the New World colonies and through the continental routes to the Baiduist heartland in [[Daras]] and [[Shansi]]. By the mid-16th century, the Canglan were among the wealthiest and most influential families in the kingdom, and their Baiduist faith and eastern coast identity placed them in direct opposition to the Tianist establishment of the western interior and the capital at [[Zhongyuan]].

[[Foyuan of Zong|Canglan Rui]] (蒼蘭瑞), the head of the house, was a charismatic and ambitious leader who had built a power base among the Baiduist merchant houses, the eastern port cities, and the dispossessed peasants of the western provinces. He presented himself as a champion of the people — a Baiduist king who would address the surplus crisis, relieve the economic suffering of the producing classes, and replace the Tianist order with a more equitable and pluralistic system.

=== Seizure of the throne ===

As the Great Peasant Revolt continued to destabilise the kingdom, Canglan Rui mobilised his supporters — Baiduist militias, eastern coast merchant levies, and Sanese mercenaries from the colonial garrisons — and marched on the capital at [[Zhongyuan]]. [[Tianhao of Zong|Emperor Tianhao]], whose military was depleted and whose political authority had collapsed, was unable to mount an effective defence. The emperor was deposed and the [[House of Yuanlong]] was removed from the throne for the first time in over three centuries. Tianhao fled across the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the [[Louyang]] colony, where he established a court-in-exile and joined the Yuanlong loyalist resistance.

Canglan Rui proclaimed himself Emperor Foyuan (佛元, "Buddhist Origin"), deliberately breaking the Tian- (天, "Heaven") naming convention that had been used by every Yuanlong emperor since the founding of the dynasty. The regnal name signalled his rejection of the Tianist theological framework that had ordained the emperor's legitimacy and his intention to establish a new order based on Baiduist principles.

The usurpation was not a coup d'état conducted in the shadows but a public transformation of the state. Foyuan convened the [[Societal Assembly]] — now dominated by his supporters, after the Tianist aristocrats had fled or been purged — and received its endorsement for his accession. The assembly's confirmation gave the usurpation a veneer of constitutional legitimacy, though the absence of the Tianist establishment and the presence of armed Baiduist militias made the nature of the endorsement clear.

== Reign of Emperor Foyuan (c. 1585–1589) ==

=== Religious reforms ===

Foyuan's four-year reign was defined by the abrupt and sweeping dismantling of the Tianist establishment. The Tianist church, which had ordained the emperor's legitimacy and staffed the administrative apparatus of the state, was not merely reformed but scrapped. Tianist temples were closed or converted to Baiduist use, Tianist clerics were removed from their positions in the court and the provinces, and the Tianist church's lands and revenues were confiscated and redistributed to Baiduist monasteries. The Tianist concept of the Mandate of Heaven — the idea that the emperor's authority was ordained by the cosmic order — was formally abolished and replaced with a Baiduist framework in which the emperor's legitimacy rested on his adherence to the Dharma and his protection of the Baiduist community.

The speed and totality of the reforms provoked immediate outrage among the Tianist elite. The northern and eastern aristocracy, which had been stripped of its land and privileges, organised an armed resistance that began within months of Foyuan's accession. Tianist militias conducted guerrilla campaigns in the eastern and northern provinces, attacking Baiduist institutions and Foyuan's officials. The Tianist church, driven underground, maintained a network of clandestine temples and ordination programmes. The Societal Assembly, reconstituted to include Baiduist representatives, was boycotted by the remaining Tianist aristocrats, who regarded the body as illegitimate.

=== Economic policies ===

Foyuan attempted to address the surplus crisis through a programme of economic mandates. Rather than imposing tariffs, the court issued direct orders halting the import of colonial goods from the New World that competed with domestic production. The mandates were designed to protect domestic farmers and artisans by cutting off the flow of spices, cotton, sugar, and other colonial commodities that had crashed domestic prices. The halts were enforced through port closures and the seizure of cargo vessels attempting to land colonial goods in violation of the mandates.

The economic mandates, while popular among the domestic producers who had been devastated by the surplus crisis, alienated the colonial administration and the colonial merchant houses. The [[Louyang]] and [[Sinchew]] colonies, whose entire economies were built on the export of plantation goods to the Zong mainland, were cut off from their primary market. The colonial authorities, already strained by the indigenous wars and the cost of maintaining garrisons, faced economic collapse as the flow of revenue from the mainland trade was severed. The colonial merchant houses — many of which had supported the usurpation — found that the regime they had installed was now destroying the commercial system that had made them wealthy.

The colonial alienation had strategic consequences. The Louyang and Sinchew colonial administrations, concluding that Foyuan's economic policies threatened the survival of the colonial enterprise, began lending covert support to the Yuanlong reclamation army in the eastern and northern provinces. The colonies provided the loyalist coalition with supplies, intelligence, and — critically — access to the Sanese mercenary garrisons that would prove decisive in the restoration campaign. The colonial support for the reclamation was a decisive factor in the coalition's ability to mount a sustained military campaign against Foyuan's forces.

The court also attempted to redistribute land and resources from the Tianist aristocracy to the dispossessed peasants who had formed the base of the Great Peasant Revolt. The land seizures were popular among the rural population but alienated the northern and eastern aristocracy and the Tianist establishment, which viewed the confiscations as an existential threat.

=== Foreign relations ===

Foyuan's Baiduist regime also reshaped Zong's diplomatic posture. The most significant change was the normalisation of relations with [[Daras]], the Baiduist heartland and the continental power that had imposed the trade blockade on Zong nearly two centuries earlier. The Darasian [[Chengzhi Dynasty]], which had long viewed Zong through the lens of the Jade Sea War and the continental rivalry, recognised in Foyuan's usurpation an opportunity to reframe the relationship. Diplomatic exchanges were initiated, and the court at Zhongyuan signalled its willingness to reopen the continental trade routes that had been severed since the Darasian blockade. The normalisation was celebrated by the Baiduist communities on both sides of the frontier as the end of two centuries of continental isolation.

The normalisation with Daras, however, came at the cost of the relationship with [[Bingzhou]], the southwestern Luoyan state that had been in personal union with Zong since the Golden Age. By the late 16th century, Bingzhou was ruled by a Baiduist sovereign — a reflection of the religion's spread across the Luoyan states through the continental missionary networks — but the Bingzhou court had consistently supported the Tianist Yuanlong regime. The Bingzhou ruler, though Baiduist in faith, was Tianist in political loyalty: the personal union with Zong, the institutional ties between the Bingzhou and Zong courts, and the shared interest in continental stability had bound Bingzhou to the Yuanlong dynasty regardless of the religious affiliation of its sovereign.

Foyuan's regime, which had scrapped the Tianist order and installed a Baiduist state, viewed Bingzhou's Baiduist ruler as a natural ally. The overtures from Zhongyuan, however, were rebuffed. The Bingzhou court, alarmed by the overthrow of the Yuanlong dynasty and the dismantling of the Tianist church, refused to recognise Foyuan's legitimacy and declared its support for the Tianist loyalist resistance. The alienation of Bingzhou was a significant diplomatic blow: the Luoyan state was a continental ally, a buffer against Darasian pressure, and a source of intelligence and supply for the loyalist coalition. Bingzhou's support for the reclamation army — despite its ruler's Baiduist faith — demonstrated that the fault lines of the conflict were political and dynastic rather than purely religious, and it deprived Foyuan's regime of a potential continental ally that might have compensated for the alienation of the colonies.

=== The Secret Trade ===

{{Main|The Secret Trade}}

During Foyuan's reign, the [[Secret Trade]] — an illicit trade in enslaved peoples — expanded significantly. Zong merchant [[Ong Sam]] (1545–1620), who had visited the Burawi colony of [[Lacashe]] in 1572 and observed the plantation system, had established a plantation operation in [[Sinchew]] and begun utilising pirates and mercenaries from the Sanese archipelago to kidnap peoples from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]] for transport to Sinchew as enslaved labour. The trade operated in a legal grey area: the Zong Edict of 1500 banned slavery on the mainland, but its applicability to overseas colonies was ambiguous.

The Secret Trade supplied the growing plantation economy of Sinchew with enslaved labour, generating wealth that flowed back to Zong and enriched the colonial merchant elite. The trade would continue until diplomatic pressure from the [[Sultanate of Maros]] led to Zong naval intervention in 1680.

=== The divided kingdom ===

Foyuan's usurpation produced a de facto partition of the Kingdom of Zong. The Baiduist-controlled lands — the western and southern provinces, where the Great Peasant Revolt had been strongest and where the Baiduist militias had displaced the imperial administration — were governed by Foyuan's court from the capital at [[Zhongyuan]]. The Tianist loyalist lands — the eastern and northern provinces, where the Tianist aristocracy had retreated and maintained the old order — were administered by a parallel government that rejected Foyuan's legitimacy and continued to operate under the Tianist framework. The deposed Emperor Tianhao, having fled across the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the [[Louyang]] colony, established a court-in-exile in the colonial capital at [[Anping]] and coordinated the loyalist resistance from abroad.

The division was not a formal partition. On the surface, the Kingdom of Zong remained a single entity in international matters. The colonial administration in the New World continued to report to Zhongyuan, and the Zong colonial authorities maintained a unified posture in dealings with the Illypnian powers and the other Fosian states. The An-Ming Understanding, the colonial trade, and the diplomatic relations with the continental powers were all conducted as if the kingdom were intact. The facade of unity was maintained partly out of necessity — the colonial enterprise could not function if the international community perceived Zong as fractured — and partly out of the peculiar nature of the usurpation, which had transformed the state without formally dissolving it.

Behind the facade, however, the division was real and deep. The Baiduist-controlled west and south were governed under Foyuan's religious reforms, with the Tianist church dismantled and Baiduist institutions elevated in its place. The Tianist loyalist east and north maintained the old order, with the Tianist church operating openly, the Tianist aristocracy in control of the administration, and the Yuanlong court-in-exile collecting taxes, maintaining militias, and preparing for the restoration. The two zones were separated by a fluid and contested frontier where skirmishes between Baiduist and Tianist militias were frequent. In Louyang, Tianhao's court-in-exile provided the symbolic legitimacy of the Yuanlong dynasty, while the colonial administration — alienated by Foyuan's economic mandates — offered the material and military resources for the reclamation.

Tianhao's elder son, [[Tianren of Zong|Tianren]], who had been sent ahead to the eastern and northern provinces to rally the loyalist forces on the mainland, came of age in the resistance and emerged as the military leader of the loyalist coalition. Within two years, the coalition — strengthened by colonial support coordinated through Tianhao's court-in-exile in Louyang — was strong enough to march on the capital.

== Global context ==

The internal crisis of Zong unfolded against a backdrop of global upheaval that shaped the kingdom's strategic environment.

=== The Sengoku Export and the Sea Wolves ===

{{Main|Okaiken|Sengoku Period}}

The continuous warfare of the Sanese archipelago throughout the [[Sengoku Period]] (1467–1582) and the subsequent [[Seto-Tosa Period]] (1582–1625) was the direct source of the Sanese mercenaries who garrisoned Zong's colonial outposts. The [[Sea Wolves]] — dispossessed samurai who had crossed the [[Shendan Ocean]] — were a crucial military resource for the Zong colonial administration, but they also introduced a volatile and semi-autonomous element into the colonial society.

The escalation of the Sengoku wars in the late 16th century — including the [[Burning of Mount Hiei]] (1584) and the [[Honto-ji Incident]] (1592) — produced a fresh wave of refugees and warriors who crossed the ocean to the Zong colonies. The establishment of the [[Honda Shogunate]] in 1601 would eventually stabilise the Sanese archipelago and affect the flow of mercenaries, but during Foyuan's reign, the Sengoku Export was at its peak.

=== The War of the Mesogean (1590–1610) ===

{{Main|War of the Mesogean}}

The [[War of the Mesogean]] (1590–1610), a prolonged conflict between the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] over control of the Mesogean Sea, diverted both powers from New World colonisation. The war weakened Illypnian colonial capacity at precisely the time when Zong was vulnerable, creating a window of opportunity for the Fosian powers to consolidate their colonial positions. The decisive [[Battle of Reige]] (1605), in which the Burawi fleet lost approximately half its ships, crippled Burawa's naval power and further reduced the Illypnian threat to the Zong colonies.

=== Darasian colonial expansion ===

The [[Chengzhi Dynasty]] of [[Daras]], having spent the 16th century rebuilding its power, began constructing a blue-water navy and dispatching colonial expeditions to the New World. Darasian settlements were established in southern [[Lurandia]] — directly within the Zong sphere of influence. The Darasian colonial expansion was a key motivating factor for the [[An-Ming Understanding]], as Zong sought to formally claim all of Lurandia and address the Darasian presence.

=== The Sultanate of Maros ===

{{Main|Sultanate of Maros}}

The [[Sultanate of Maros]], an Islamic thalassocracy on the [[Balausia Peninsula]], underwent a conversion to Islam (1540–1590) and emerged as a Fosian colonial competitor. In 1577, Sultan Jalaludin II established settlements on southeastern [[Almuria]] and conducted multiple expeditions across the Shendan Ocean. Maros's emergence as a New World colonial power created both a potential rival and a potential ally for Zong, culminating in the An-Ming Understanding.

== Yuanlong restoration (1587–1589) ==

{{Main|Yuanlong Restoration}}

The Yuanlong restoration was a two-year military campaign that culminated in the overthrow of Emperor Foyuan and the return of the [[House of Yuanlong]] to the throne. The usurper's coalition, never fully consolidated, fractured rapidly under the pressure of the Tianist insurgency and the economic consequences of Foyuan's colonial trade mandates. The colonial merchant houses, which had supported the usurpation, were alienated by the halt on colonial goods imports that had severed their primary market. The [[Louyang]] and [[Sinchew]] colonial administrations, facing economic collapse, lent covert support to the reclamation army — providing supplies, intelligence, and access to the Sanese mercenary garrisons. The Baiduist monasteries, which had been granted unprecedented privileges, were increasingly seen as corrupt and exploitative by the rural population. The northern and eastern aristocracy and the Tianist church, though weakened, had maintained their organisational coherence and their commitment to restoring the Yuanlong dynasty.

[[Tianren of Zong|Tianren]], who had come of age in the eastern and northern provinces and had been groomed for the restoration by the Yuanlong loyalists, led a military campaign that combined the northern and eastern aristocratic militias, the Tianist church's underground networks, and contingents of the regular army that had remained loyal to the Yuanlong. The campaign advanced from the eastern and northern provinces toward the capital, gathering support as it progressed.

Foyuan's forces, depleted by years of guerrilla warfare and weakened by the defection of key allies, were unable to halt the advance. The decisive engagement was fought outside [[Zhongyuan]], where the Yuanlong loyalist army defeated the Baiduist forces. Foyuan was captured and executed. The [[House of Canglan]] was dissolved, its properties confiscated, and its leading members executed or exiled.

Tianren was proclaimed emperor and the [[House of Yuanlong]] was restored to the throne. The deposed Tianhao, who had survived the usurpation and the restoration campaign from his exile in Louyang, returned to the capital. The Societal Assembly was reconvened with the Tianist establishment restored to its former position, and the court began the process of reversing Foyuan's reforms.

The restoration, however, was not a simple return to the pre-usurpation order. The four years of Canglan rule had demonstrated that the imperial authority, when unconstrained, could be overthrown and replaced — and that the institutions of the state were insufficient to prevent a single ambitious house from seizing the throne. The Tianist aristocracy and the Societal Assembly, which had borne the cost of the usurpation and had organised the resistance that overthrew Foyuan, demanded institutional guarantees against a recurrence. Tianren, whose legitimacy rested on the assembly's endorsement of the restoration, was in no position to refuse.

The emperor was forced to make concessions to the [[Societal Assembly]] that altered the balance of power between the throne and the legislature. The assembly's legislative authority was expanded: the emperor's decrees on matters of taxation, religious policy, and the succession were now subject to assembly ratification, a requirement that had previously existed in theory but had been routinely ignored in practice. The assembly gained the right to convene its own sessions without the emperor's summons — a power that had been lost during the centralisation of the later Yuanlong reigns — and to initiate legislation, rather than merely deliberating on the emperor's proposals. The assembly also secured the right to audit the court's expenditure of colonial revenue, addressing the fiscal opacity that had enabled Foyuan's rise.

The concessions did not transform Zong into a constitutional monarchy. The emperor retained the power to appoint and dismiss ministers, to command the military, to conduct foreign policy, and to issue edicts on matters of public order and defence without assembly consent. The Tianist church remained the state religion, and the emperor's legitimacy continued to rest on the Tianist Mandate of Heaven. The assembly's new powers were legislative and fiscal, not executive, and the emperor's authority to govern remained substantial. But the shift was real: for the first time since the founding of the dynasty, the assembly possessed institutional mechanisms to constrain the throne, and the emperor was required to govern in consultation with a body that represented the aristocratic and commercial elites rather than ruling through them.

The expansion of the assembly's authority also changed its composition. The inclusion of Baiduist representatives under Tianren's integration policy brought non-Tianist voices into the legislature for the first time. The colonial administrations of [[Louyang]] and [[Sinchew]], which had lent critical support to the restoration through supplies, intelligence, and Sanese mercenary garrisons, demanded and received representation in the assembly — the first time the overseas colonies were granted seats in the metropolitan legislature. The colonial representatives, drawn from the colonial merchant elite and the plantation administration, brought the interests of the New World settlements into the Zong political process and created a permanent constituency for the colonial trade within the assembly. The body that Tianren convened was larger, more diverse, and more assertive than the assembly that had served the earlier Yuanlong emperors, and its expanded role would shape the politics of the expulsion period and beyond.

== Tianren and the road to expulsion (1589–1606) ==

The restoration of the Yuanlong dynasty did not resolve the tensions that had produced the usurpation; it transformed them. The Tianist establishment, which had spent four years underground and had suffered the confiscation of its property, the suppression of its religion, and the execution of its leaders, returned to power with a thirst for retribution. The Baiduist communities, which had been elevated to a position of state favour under Foyuan and had participated in the institutions of the usurper's regime, were now viewed not merely as religious competitors but as traitors who had enabled the overthrow of the dynasty.

=== Tianren's policy of integration ===

Tianren, however, did not share the Tianist hardliners' appetite for retribution. The emperor was acutely aware that the Baiduist merchant houses of the eastern coast controlled the Shendan trade networks and the colonial commercial infrastructure that sustained the kingdom's economy. The surplus crisis had demonstrated the dangers of disrupting the commercial system, and the colonial enterprise depended on the expertise, capital, and maritime networks of the eastern merchant elite — many of whom were Baiduist. A wholesale assault on the Baiduist community, Tianren reasoned, would destroy the economic foundation of the kingdom.

Instead, Tianren pursued a policy of integration and reform. The court sought to reincorporate the Baiduist communities into the restored Yuanlong order by offering them a regulated but legitimate place within the Tianist framework. Baiduist merchants were confirmed in their commercial privileges, Baiduist monasteries were permitted to continue operating under state supervision, and Baiduist representatives were offered seats in the [[Societal Assembly]] — provided they renounced any connection to the Foyuan regime and affirmed the supremacy of the Tianist church. The court simultaneously attempted to address the surplus crisis through a programme of economic reform that combined targeted tariffs on colonial goods with investment in domestic production.

The policy was controversial. The Tianist hardliners in the court and the western aristocracy viewed any accommodation of Baiduism as a betrayal of the restoration and an insult to the memory of the Tianist martyrs who had died under Foyuan's persecution. They argued that the Baiduist communities could not be trusted, that their ties to Darasian Baiduist institutions made them a permanent security threat, and that the usurpation had proven that coexistence was impossible. The hardliners pressed for the complete elimination of Baiduist influence from the kingdom.

=== The Daaiho War and the Jyunhap collapse (1594–1608) ===

{{Main|Daaiho War}}

The integration policy was further complicated by a catastrophe unfolding on the continental frontier. The [[Daaiho War]] (大河戰爭, "Great River War") erupted in the [[Shansi]] states — the Sansei-speaking region to the south of Zong that comprised the [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]] federation. The war was triggered by the merger of the two largest Jyunhap states — the Wong State and the Leung State — under a marriage alliance that created a single dominant power within the federation. The smaller states, alarmed by the concentration of power, sought to leave the Jyunhap federation. The merged Wong-Leung State, seeking to maintain the federation under its own leadership, demanded a greater tax share from the member states to fund a collective defence against both the secession movement and the external pressures of Darasian expansion. The smaller states refused, the federation's collective institutions collapsed, and the Shansi interior descended into a multi-sided war fought along the great river systems of the region. The war began in 1594 and raged for over a decade.

The Daaiho War caused the collapse of the Jyunhap federation. The loose confederation of princely states, which had maintained a fragile collective security arrangement for over two centuries, disintegrated under the pressure of the war. Individual states turned on one another, alliances shifted unpredictably, and the federation's collective institutions ceased to function. The collapse of the Jyunhap removed the buffer that had shielded Zong's southern frontier and eliminated a continental ally that had supported Zong since the Jade Sea War.

The most immediate consequence for Zong was the migration of millions of Shansi refugees into southern Zong. The war produced a flood of displaced civilians — farmers, artisans, merchants, and entire communities — who crossed the frontier into the Zong provinces seeking safety from the fighting. The migration, which began in the mid-1590s and continued through 1608, placed an enormous strain on the southern provinces, which were already struggling with the aftermath of the surplus crisis and the Great Peasant Revolt. The refugees were predominantly Baiduist — Shansi had been a major centre of Baiduism for centuries — and their arrival dramatically increased the Baiduist population of southern Zong.

The migration also produced a schism within the Baiduist community. The Shansi Baiduists, who had their own monastic traditions, organisational structures, and theological emphases, differed from the Zong Baiduists who had been part of the eastern coast merchant culture for generations. The two communities shared a religion but not a culture, and the influx of Shansi Baiduists into southern Zong created tensions over resources, authority, and doctrinal practice. The Zong Baiduist establishment, centred on the eastern monasteries, viewed the Shansi newcomers as a disruptive influence that would provoke Tianist retaliation. The Shansi Baiduists, many of whom had lost everything in the Daaiho War, were more radical, more militant, and more willing to confront the Tianist state. The factionalism between the Jyunhap Baiduists and the Zong Baiduists would prove to be a critical factor in the failure of the Canglan Rebellion.

=== The Canglan Rebellion (c. 1605) ===

{{Main|Canglan Rebellion}}

The hardliners' arguments were dramatically vindicated by the outbreak of the [[Canglan Rebellion]] in c. 1605. A conspiracy, centred on the surviving remnants of the [[House of Canglan]] and a network of Baiduist merchant houses and monasteries in the eastern port cities, launched an armed uprising against the Tianren court. The rebels attempted to restore the Canglan dynasty by seizing the capital and assassinating the emperor.

The rebellion's military force was formed in the newly breakaway [[Lau Kingdom]] (1605), one of the Shansi states that had declared independence from the collapsing Jyunhap. The Lau Kingdom, situated on the southern fringe of the Shansi theatre, provided the rebellion with a base of operations, a supply of arms, and a population of displaced Shansi warriors who had fled the Daaiho War. The Canglan conspirators, working through the Baiduist networks that connected Shansi to the Zong eastern coast, coordinated with the Lau Kingdom to assemble an army.

The coalition, however, was fatally divided. The rebel forces were split between loyalty to the [[House of Canglan]] and loyalty to the Lau King, who had his own ambitions in the Shansi power vacuum and viewed the Canglan restoration as a means to secure his own position rather than an end in itself. The Shansi Baiduists who formed the bulk of the rebel army were motivated by the grievances of the Daaiho War and the persecution they had fled, not by devotion to the Canglan dynasty. The Zong Baiduists, who had supported the original usurpation, were wary of the Shansi newcomers and reluctant to commit their resources to a campaign led by a Lau King they did not trust. The factionalism between the Jyunhap Baiduists and the Zong Baiduists undermined the cohesion of the rebellion from within.

Despite these divisions, the rebels succeeded in assassinating [[Tianren of Zong|Emperor Tianren]] — who had staked his reign on the policy of integration and who had trusted the Baiduist communities to coexist within the Tianist order. Tianren's murder was a devastating blow to the Yuanlong dynasty. The emperor, who had survived the usurpation and led the restoration, was killed by the same Baiduist forces that his own policy of tolerance had sought to accommodate.

The assassination, however, did not produce the political collapse the conspirators had intended. The rebel army, weakened by the factionalism between the Canglan loyalists and the Lau King's forces, was unable to capitalise on the assassination. The divided command structure, the mistrust between Shansi and Zong Baiduists, and the conflicting ambitions of the rebellion's leaders prevented the coordinated advance on the capital that the plan required. The rebellion was suppressed within months of Tianren's death, its forces scattered, and the surviving Canglan conspirators executed or driven into exile. The Lau Kingdom, deprived of its Zongese allies, was left to face the consequences of the Daaiho War alone.

The court's intelligence revealed that the conspiracy had reached into the highest levels of the eastern merchant elite, including Baiduist representatives who had been seated in the Societal Assembly under Tianren's own integration policy. The discovery that the Baiduist communities had exploited the very institutions of coexistence to plot the overthrow of the dynasty — and that the factionalism within the Baiduist community had not prevented the assassination but had only prevented the rebellion's broader success — discredited the integration policy entirely.

=== Tianxiao's accession and the turn to expulsion ===

[[Tianxiao of Zong|Tianxiao]], Tianren's younger brother, inherited the throne in the aftermath of the assassination. Tianxiao had not shared his brother's optimism about Baiduist integration, and the murder of Tianren confirmed his convictions. The new emperor, convinced that the Baiduist communities could not be integrated and that any tolerance would be exploited, reversed the integration policy immediately. The Tianist hardliners, now dominant in the court, argued that the only solution was the complete removal of Baiduism from the kingdom. Tianxiao accepted their argument.

The escalating tensions were compounded by the geopolitical context. The Darasian colonial expansion into southern Lurandia, the rise of the Sultanate of Maros, and the ongoing War of the Mesogean all contributed to a sense of strategic vulnerability. The Baiduist communities' ties to Darasian Baiduist institutions were viewed with renewed suspicion, and the foreign Baiduist presence in the eastern ports was treated as a security threat.

=== The An-Ming Understanding (1605–1606) ===

{{Main|An-Ming Understanding}}

In 1605–1606, the Zong court, represented by diplomats [[Lim Ming]] and [[Chen An]], negotiated the [[An-Ming Understanding]] with the [[Sultanate of Maros]] in the port city of [[Haihia]]. The Understanding consisted of three conventions that formally partitioned the New World: [[Lurandia]] was assigned to the Zong sphere, [[Abyala]] to the Marosan sphere, and the [[Liaojie isthmus]] was designated as a neutral zone. The convention explicitly claimed that Darasian colonies in southern Lurandia fell within the Zong sphere.

The An-Ming Understanding represented the formal entry of eastern Fosian states into organised, treaty-bound colonial competition with Western powers. It directly conflicted with the [[Abyala Charter]] (1537) between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]], establishing a competing legal framework for New World partition. The Understanding was a diplomatic triumph for the Zong court, but it also heightened the sense that the Baiduist communities — with their foreign ties and their continental connections — were a potential fifth column in the kingdom's strategic position.

== The 1606 decree ==

{{Main|Baiduist Expulsion from Zong}}

In 1606, [[Tianxiao of Zong|Emperor Tianxiao]] convened the [[Societal Assembly]] and issued the decree that would become the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong]]. The decree banned the practice of [[Baiduism]] within the kingdom, ordered the expulsion of all foreign Baiduists, and mandated the forced relocation of Zong Baiduists to the [[Sinchew]] colony. The decree was the culmination of the [[Canglan Rebellion]] — the assassination of Emperor Tianren had demonstrated that the Baiduist communities, despite a decade of integration and reform, continued to plot the overthrow of the dynasty. Tianxiao, who had inherited the throne after his brother's murder, chose the survival of the Yuanlong dynasty over the presence of Baiduism in the kingdom.

The expulsion triggered the [[Baidu People's Rebellion]] (1606–1623), a series of Baiduist uprisings that resulted in over 100,000 deaths and has been characterised by modern historians as a genocide. By 1650, over one million Zong Baiduists had been transported to Sinchew.

== Society and culture ==

The decades of crisis, war, and religious upheaval left a deep imprint on the cultural life of Zong. The period produced transformations in literature, architecture, religious practice, and popular culture that would outlast the political turmoil that created them.

=== Literature and the literature of crisis ===

The surplus crisis and the Great Peasant Revolt produced a new body of literature that broke from the court-centric traditions of the earlier Shendan period. The popular verse forms of the eastern coast, which had previously celebrated the oceanic voyage and the wonders of the New World, turned to themes of dispossession, famine, and social collapse. A genre of folk poetry — sometimes called the "Crisis Songs" (危歌) — emerged among the dispossessed peasants of the western and southern provinces, combining Baiduist imagery of suffering and deliverance with laments for the collapsed agrarian economy. The Crisis Songs were transmitted orally through the Baiduist monastery networks and were not committed to writing until the early 17th century, when Tianist scholars began collecting them as evidence of the social disorder that had produced the usurpation.

The court literature of the period reflected the political fractures. Under Foyuan's reign, the court commissioned Baiduist chronicles that reframed the history of the Yuanlong dynasty as a period of Tianist oppression and presented the Canglan usurpation as a deliverance. After the Yuanlong restoration, the Tianist establishment produced its own counter-chronicles that emphasised the suffering of the Tianist community under Foyuan and the martyrdom of the clerics who had resisted the usurpation. The two bodies of literature — Baiduist and Tianist — offered irreconcilable narratives of the same events, and the competition between them would shape Zong historiography for decades.

The Daaiho War and the Shansi refugee migration introduced Sansei-language literature to southern Zong. Shansi bards, who had carried the oral traditions of the Jyunhap states across the frontier, performed epic cycles recounting the great river battles and the fall of the Jyunhap federation. The Sansei epics, performed in the refugee quarters of the southern cities, attracted both Baiduist and Tianist audiences and introduced Zong audiences to a literary tradition that had previously been confined to the Shansi interior. The influence of Sansei verse forms on southern Zong poetry would persist well beyond the expulsion period.

=== Architecture and the built environment ===

The period produced distinctive architectural changes in the Zong cities. The eastern port cities, enriched by the Shendan trade, had developed a colonial-influenced architectural style that incorporated tropical hardwoods, New World motifs, and the open-air courtyard designs suited to the maritime climate. The surplus crisis and the revolt interrupted the construction boom, and many of the grand merchant houses of the eastern coast were damaged or destroyed during the fighting.

Foyuan's brief reign left an architectural mark. The Baiduist regime converted a number of Tianist temples to Baiduist use, altering their interior layouts to accommodate Baiduist worship practices. After the restoration, the Tianist church reconsecrated the temples and attempted to reverse the alterations, but some of the Baiduist modifications — particularly the removal of the Tianist altar screens and the opening of the central halls to communal gathering — proved difficult to undo and were quietly retained in several buildings.

The Shansi refugee migration produced a more visible architectural change. The refugees, settling in the southern provinces, built temporary shelters and prayer halls using the construction techniques of the Shansi interior — rammed-earth walls, tiled roofs, and the enclosed courtyard plans that were suited to the Sansei climate but unfamiliar in southern Zong. The Shansi quarter of the southern cities developed a distinctive architectural character that contrasted with the Zong and colonial-influenced styles of the surrounding districts. The Shansi prayer halls, which served as both religious centres and community gathering places for the refugees, were among the first Baiduist institutions in southern Zong that were not connected to the eastern monastery network.

=== Firearms and the transformation of warfare ===

The introduction of Darasian matchlock muskets during the Great Peasant Revolt transformed not only the military balance but also the cultural understanding of warfare in Zong. Prior to the revolt, gunpowder weapons had been associated with the state — siege cannons, naval artillery, and the hand cannons of the imperial garrisons were symbols of the court's military authority. The Baiduist militias' use of matchlock muskets democratized firearm technology, placing infantry weapons in the hands of non-state actors for the first time in Zong history.

The cultural impact was profound. The matchlock musket became a symbol of the Baiduist revolt — a weapon that had enabled the dispossessed to challenge the imperial army and that had demonstrated the vulnerability of the Tianist military order. After the restoration, the court attempted to restrict the private ownership of firearms, but the weapons had already spread through the Baiduist networks and could not be fully recalled. The colonial garrisons, which had previously held a monopoly on gunpowder weaponry, found that the technology had diffused to the mainland militias and to the indigenous forces of the New World, the latter supplied by Volisanian smuggling through the Balboa colony.

The diffusion of firearms also changed the literature of warfare. The earlier colonial chronicles, which had described the Zong military's superiority in siege and naval weaponry, gave way to a new literature that grappled with the implications of infantry firearms — the levelling of the battlefield, the vulnerability of the traditional cavalry charge, and the new relationship between technology and social power that the matchlock had introduced.

=== Religious culture and the Baiduist-Tianist divide ===

The religious culture of the period was defined by the intensifying polarisation between Baiduism and Tianism. The Baiduist monasteries of the eastern coast, which had functioned as commercial and educational institutions as well as religious ones, expanded their cultural role during the surplus crisis by providing charity, schooling, and community organisation that the Tianist state had ceased to offer. The Baiduist monastic schools taught literacy, numeracy, and the Baiduist canon to a population that the Tianist educational system had abandoned, and the graduates of these schools formed the administrative cadres of the Baiduist parallel government that emerged during the Great Peasant Revolt.

The Tianist church, driven underground during Foyuan's reign, developed its own cultural forms. The clandestine temples of the resistance period produced a body of devotional literature — the "Underground Sutras" (地下經) — that combined Tianist theology with the language of martyrdom and resistance. The Underground Sutras were copied by hand and circulated secretly through the Tianist networks of the eastern and northern provinces, and they became the foundational texts of the post-restoration Tianist church, which viewed them as proof of the church's suffering and resilience.

The Shansi refugee migration introduced a third religious cultural tradition. The Shansi Baiduists, who had developed their own monastic practices in the Jyunhap states, brought a distinctive form of Baiduist worship that emphasised communal chanting, river-based purification rituals, and a more egalitarian monastic structure than the hierarchical eastern coast establishments. The differences between Shansi and Zong Baiduism were not merely theological — they were cultural, and the collision of the two traditions in the refugee quarters of southern Zong produced both creative synthesis and bitter factionalism.

=== Colonial society and the Sinchew frontier ===

The colonial society of the period developed its own cultural forms, distinct from the mainland. The Sinchew colony, with its frontier character, its Sanese mercenary population, and its plantation economy, produced a creole culture that blended Zong, Sanese, and indigenous elements. The Sanese mercenaries of the Sea Wolves brought their own martial traditions, religious practices (particularly Iron Baiduism), and material culture to the colonial frontier. The interaction between the Sanese warrior culture, the Zong colonial administration, and the indigenous peoples of the northwestern coast produced a society that was increasingly distinct from the mainland.

The Secret Trade and the plantation economy of Sinchew also shaped the colonial culture. The enslaved peoples brought from Dagit and the Balausia Peninsula introduced their own cultural traditions — music, food preparation, oral storytelling, and religious practices — into the Sinchew plantation society. The cultural exchange between the enslaved population, the Sanese mercenaries, the Zong colonists, and the indigenous peoples of the region produced a colonial creole culture that would later be transformed by the mass arrival of expelled Zong Baiduists after 1606.

== Legacy ==

The period from 1538 to 1606 transformed Zong from a colonial power into a state in crisis. The surplus crisis, the peasant revolt, the Baiduist usurpation, and the Yuanlong restoration created a cycle of political instability and religious violence that culminated in the Baiduist Expulsion. The expulsion, in turn, transformed the [[Sinchew]] colony from a penal settlement into a major population centre and reshaped the demographic and religious landscape of the Zong colonial empire.

The period also marked the integration of the Zong colonial enterprise into the wider global system. The Great Disappointment, the An-Ming Understanding, and the Secret Trade connected the Zong colonies to the Illypnian and Fosian colonial spheres in ways that would shape the trajectory of the New World for centuries.

